From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz M <lukasz1992m+openwrt@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net airoha AN7563 FE QDMA PPE layout and settings as on stock

AN7563 was handled with the AN7583 QDMA and PPE sizes and the mainline
EN7581 front-end settings.  This patch gives it its own SoC data and
programs the front end, both QDMAs and the PPE the way the stock TP-Link
XX530v V2 (AN7552 SDK) does.  Every value below was read back from the
running stock unit or taken from the vendor driver named.

Layout (the kernel lost its page tables with the AN7583 sizes):
- QDMA: 16 rx and 8 tx rings, two interrupt registers per bank, rings
  10-14 on irq bank 1; no access to rx rings 16-31 (0xe00), tx rings 8-31
  (0xb00) or interrupt registers 2-4 (0x720-0x750), which the stock
  driver never touches (qdmaGetIntMask(base, bank, 1..2) only).
- Two GDM ports (no GDM3/GDM4 setup), no hw LRO, LMGR descriptors in
  DRAM and 1 KiB HWFWD payloads on both QDMAs ((LMGR_INIT_CFG & 0x1ffff)
  << 5 bytes; X-WRT 941-02, the driver that ran on this board).
- IP fragment and reassembly rings 8 and 9 (vendor qdma_dev_7552.h
  FRAG_RX_RING_IDX/ASSE_RX_RING_IDX).
- PPE: 512 SRAM entries, PPE_TB_CFG SRAM code 7 (hw_nat ra_nat_ic_dis.h
  SUPPORT_SRAM_MAX_512 for isAN7552, foe_fdb_7552_64Byte.h).
- The TX ring is the netdev queue reduced to the SoC's rings
  (airoha_qdma_get_txq(), which the xPON TX uses too); QoS channel and
  queue in the descriptor still come from the netdev queue.
- Do not "correct" the QDMA interrupt offsets to the vendor header: for
  AN7552 qdma_reg.h falls back to qdma_reg_7516.h, which describes other
  silicon (0x700/0x704 read 0xdeadbeef here); 0x20, 0x24, 0x720, 0x724
  and 0x730 are the real registers.

PSE buffer in the GDMP SRAM (fe_ic_dis.h SUPPORT_PSE_USE_GDMP for
isAN7552): GDMP 0x74 = 3 (ecnt_gdump.c gdump_mode_sel(FE_ACCESS_DATA)),
SCU SSR3 0x94 bit 30 (PCIE_HSGMII1_SEL) cleared, PSE_FQ_CFG = 0x500
before the FE core reset (fe_api.c pse_set_total_page()), and
PSE_SHARE_USED_THD = 0x01e001f4 instead of per-port thresholds (stock
fe_core for HIR 0xc/0xf).  The "gdmp" registers (0x1fbf9000 in the
stock DTB) and the SCU ("airoha,scu") come from the DT.

Front end:
- GDM_MISC_CFG 0x1fb50148 = 0: GDM2_RDM_ACK_WAIT_PREF and
  GDM2_CHN_VLD_MODE are not set (X-WRT leaves them off as well).
- WAN_MTU0..7 0x1fb52300..0x231c = 0x3fff3fff at FE init and not touched
  on an MTU change, so double-tagged or larger frames from the OLT are
  not cut (ours was VLAN_ETH_HLEN + MTU).
- GDM long frame length 4004 on GDM1 (0x1fb50514) and 2000 on GDM2
  (0x1fb51514, the GPON frame limit) instead of AIROHA_MAX_RX_SIZE; with
  no RX scatter a frame has to fit one RX buffer anyway.
- FE_IPV6_EXT 0x1fb50014 = 0x2c: extension header 0 is the fragment
  header (fe.c fe_crsn_ipv6_fragment_init(), SUPPORT_FE_IPV6_EXT on
  AN7552 and EN7581), so the FE parses IPv6 fragments.

WAN QDMA (QDMA1, at 0x1fb56000):
- FWD_DSCP_LOW_THR 0x100 (QDMA0 0x080).
- PSE_BUF_USAGE_CFG 0x1fb57028 = 0xb000001a: the PSE buffer estimate
  (bit 29) on from the start (QDMA0 0x9000001a, off), and
  TXBUF_THRESHOLD 0x1fb570d0 total 0xc0, not only once the xPON client
  enables the estimate.
- EGRESS_RATE_METER_CFG 0xbffd40c8 (window 20, timeslice 200), QDMA0
  0xbffff7ff (window 31, timeslice 2047): the peak rate (bit 30, set out
  of reset) off on both, the equal rate (bit 17) off on the WAN QDMA.

TX congestion control, both QDMAs (vendor
qdma_set_txq_cngst_auto_config() through QDMA_API_SET_TXQ_CNGST_AUTO_
CONFIG from xpondrv.c and hw_nat): the dynamic mode was on but its
thresholds were the reset ones, no limit, so one queue or channel could
take every HWFWD buffer.  The stock BUFFER_THRESHOLD table for more than
256 descriptors (8192 here as there), half of it for DEI frames, the QoS
aging (agingEn, agingFastReplaceEn) and the DEI WRED thresholds:
  0x10a4 0x1ccc1000  0x10a8 0x10000100  0x10ac 0x10000020
  0x10d4 0x0e660800  0x10d8 0x08000080  0x10dc 0x08000010
  0x10bc 0xa000c350  0x1094 0x00421084

TX_RING_BLOCKING bit 2 (vendor TXQ_DYN_CNGSTCTL_CPU_TX_DROP_EN) on CPU TX
ring 0 only (stock QDMA +0x104 = 0x4, +0x124..+0x1e4 = 0): over a
congestion threshold rings 1-7 stall and the netdev queue stops, so the
qdisc (fq_codel, HTB) picks what to drop instead of a blind hardware tail
drop.

Signed-off-by: Lukasz M <lukasz1992m+openwrt@gmail.com>
